WEBVTT

00:00:00.246 --> 00:00:00.968
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 4

00:00:01.753 --> 00:00:03.120
Leaving prison

00:00:03.905 --> 00:00:07.313
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> At the end of their sentence, most prisoners will be released.

00:00:07.647 --> 00:00:11.165
Some may get parole and be released early for good behaviour,

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:14.524
if they agree to abide by certain conditions,

00:00:14.715 --> 00:00:19.109
such as staying within a certain area and not going out at night.

00:00:19.759 --> 00:00:23.827
They may have to wear an electronic tag, usually an ankle bracelet,

00:00:23.994 --> 00:00:25.727
which allows them to be tracked.

00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:31.549
After release, ex-offenders can find it difficult to reintegrate into society.

00:00:32.271 --> 00:00:37.126
They often have difficulty finding work, and earn less money when they do.

00:00:37.561 --> 00:00:43.696
They experience a wide range of medical and psychological issues, and many reoffend.

00:00:44.401 --> 00:00:47.735
There are cases where prisoners have managed to obtain a retrial

00:00:47.862 --> 00:00:50.568
and proved that they have been wrongfully convicted.

00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:54.742
In such cases, a pardon is granted by the government

00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:59.277
(in the USA, by the governor of the state), the sentence is quashed,

00:00:59.396 --> 00:01:02.613
and they are likely to receive substantial compensation.